{"text": "Lesson eight: The cache and the phase split, seen across the whole stack. You cannot optimize the layers in isolation. The survey's synthesis is two motifs. 1st, the key value cache is a ledger every pillar writes in. Pillar one reduces a structural dimension, fewer key value heads, or a latent width. Pillar two routes validation, tree-shaped drafts, checked under a mask. Pillar three reduces word length, down to about two-bit asymmetric grids. Pillar four allocates physical blocks, page tables, and trees for shared prefixes. These add. A real deployment can run grouped query attention, speculative trees, a four-bit cache, and page detention together. The friction is layout. Quantized caches used to be widened back to 16-bit before a stock attention kernel. Newer kernels attend directly in the low-bit layout, but every new combination of bit width, head sharing, and page size wants a new kernel. That co-design burden is the cost of the additive win. 2nd, splitting prefill from decode changes which quantization and which decoder you even want. On one GPU, a single numeric format has to serve two masters. Prefill wants 8-bit weights and 8-bit activations, or 8-bit floats, to fill the tensor cores. Decode wants narrow weights, 4-bit or even 2-bit, to cut bytes on the bus. Disaggregation makes different schemes per phase conceivable. SmoothQuant on the prefill pool, GPTQ on the decode pool. The survey is explicit that it does not know of a production system doing this yet. It is future work, not current practice. Do not over-claim it. Speculation changes the machine mix. Heavy verification pushes decode toward a compute-bound envelope, so the right ratio of prefill nodes to decode nodes moves. The new bottleneck can be the link inside the node, rather than high bandwidth memory. If you only memorize that speculation saves bandwidth, you miss this second-order effect. When two techniques stack, say which term of the cache formula each one moves, or which side of the roofline. If you cannot, they are probably not as independent as the abstracts claim.", "language": "en", "duration": 134.76, "words": [{"text": "Lesson", "start": 0.14, "end": 0.46}, {"text": "eight:", "start": 0.6, "end": 0.8}, {"text": "The", "start": 1.501, "end": 1.581}, {"text": "cache", "start": 1.641, "end": 2.061}, {"text": "and", "start": 2.141, "end": 2.221}, {"text": "the", "start": 2.241, "end": 2.301}, {"text": "phase", "start": 2.381, "end": 2.681}, {"text": "split,", "start": 2.721, "end": 3.062}, {"text": "seen", "start": 3.402, "end": 3.582}, {"text": "across", "start": 3.602, "end": 3.902}, {"text": "the", "start": 3.942, "end": 4.022}, {"text": "whole", "start": 4.062, "end": 4.262}, {"text": "stack.", "start": 4.322, "end": 4.662}, {"text": "You", "start": 5.243, "end": 5.323}, {"text": "cannot", "start": 5.343, "end": 5.643}, {"text": "optimize", "start": 5.743, "end": 6.223}, {"text": "the", "start": 6.243, "end": 6.343}, {"text": "layers", "start": 6.383, "end": 6.684}, {"text": "in", "start": 6.704, "end": 6.764}, {"text": "isolation.", "start": 6.904, "end": 7.464}, {"text": "The", "start": 8.104, "end": 8.184}, {"text": "survey's", "start": 8.244, "end": 8.665}, {"text": "synthesis", "start": 8.725, "end": 9.265}, {"text": "is", "start": 9.345, "end": 9.445}, {"text": "two", "start": 9.565, "end": 9.765}, {"text": "motifs.", "start": 9.805, "end": 10.365}, {"text": "First,", "start": 11.006, "end": 11.386}, {"text": "the", "start": 11.806, "end": 11.886}, {"text": "key", "start": 11.926, "end": 12.086}, {"text": "value", "start": 12.166, "end": 12.507}, {"text": "cache", "start": 12.547, "end": 12.907}, {"text": "is", "start": 12.967, "end": 13.047}, {"text": "a", "start": 13.087, "end": 13.107}, {"text": "ledger", "start": 13.207, "end": 13.507}, {"text": "every", "start": 13.667, "end": 13.887}, {"text": "pillar", "start": 13.987, "end": 14.247}, {"text": "writes", "start": 14.287, "end": 14.588}, {"text": "in.", "start": 14.668, "